# terraform/

The state that lives *outside* the cluster: the public DNS zone, the SSO object
inventory{% if vpn_tailscale %}, the tailnet policy{% endif %}{% if use_unifi %}, and the gateway's own networks{% endif %}. Everything
inside the cluster is Flux's, and nothing here creates a Kubernetes object.

| Module | Manages | State name | Apply |
|---|---|---|---|
| `cloudflare/` | Zone settings + the records external-dns cannot own | `cloudflare` | `task terraform:apply` |
{% if vpn_tailscale %}| `tailscale/` | Tailnet ACL, SSH rules, route auto-approvers, Split-DNS | `tailscale` | supervised |
{% endif %}| `authentik/` | OIDC/proxy/SAML providers, applications, groups, bindings | `authentik` | supervised |
{% if use_unifi %}| `unifi/` | Gateway networks and VLANs, firewall zones and policies, WLANs, port forwards | `unifi` | supervised |
{% endif %}
Each root module is a thin consumer: the resources, defaults and guardrails come
from a `weisssrv-lib//terraform/modules/<name>` module pinned by `?ref=` in its
`main.tf`, and the site data — records, policy, object inventory — is a separate
file in the module directory. Upgrading the platform is a ref bump plus a plan.

## Conventions

- **The provider block, the backend and the lockfile belong to the root module.**
  A reusable module can declare neither a provider nor a backend, which is why
  each directory here carries its own `versions.tf`.
- **One state per module.** They fail independently, they are applied
  independently, and a shared state would serialize unrelated changes.
- **Commit `.terraform.lock.hcl`.** It pins the exact provider builds; a fresh
  `init` without it can silently pick up a newer patch.
- **No `-auto-approve` for the SSO{% if vpn_tailscale %}, tailnet{% endif %}{% if use_unifi %} or gateway{% endif %}
  module.** Those tasks refuse the flag: a bad apply locks users out{% if vpn_tailscale %},
  severs remote access{% endif %}{% if use_unifi %} or leaves a LAN nobody can reach the console
  from{% endif %}, and the plan review is the control.

## State backend

State is GitLab-managed (`backend "http"`), configured entirely through
`TF_HTTP_*` environment variables — CI uses `CI_JOB_TOKEN`, local runs use the
project access token from the secret store. Nothing is configured in HCL, so the
same module works in both places.

The address is built from `{{ git_host }}` and the URL-encoded project path, so
no numeric project ID has to be looked up. If your instance rejects the path
form, pass the numeric ID: `task terraform:init TF_STATE_PROJECT=<id>`.

Moving to another state backend (S3, Terraform Cloud) means replacing the
`backend "http" {}` block in each `versions.tf` and the `TF_HTTP_*` blocks in
the Taskfile and CI — the modules themselves do not care.

## Reaching the library

`terraform init` fetches each module over HTTPS from the library repository. If
that repository is private, give git a credential for it (a credential helper or
`~/.netrc` entry) or switch the sources to `git::ssh://`. This is also why
`task terraform:validate` needs network access.

In CI the credential is the pipeline's own job token: every Terraform job sets
`GIT_CONFIG_*` so that `https://{{ git_host }}/` is rewritten to a
`gitlab-ci-token:${CI_JOB_TOKEN}@` URL for the duration of the job. That token
only works against the instance that issued it, and only if the library project
allows this project in its job-token allowlist (or is public). Without one of
those, the first Terraform pipeline fails at `init` with a git authentication
error, not a Terraform one.

## Plan, apply, drift

| Job | When | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `terraform-plan` | MRs and `main`, on `terraform/cloudflare/**` | Plans, saves `tfplan`, publishes the MR widget report |
| `deploy-terraform` | manual on `main` | Applies **that saved plan**, not a fresh one |
{% if vpn_tailscale %}| `tailscale-drift-plan` | schedule, and `main` on tailscale changes | Read-only plan, advisory |
{% endif %}| `authentik-drift-plan` | schedule, and `main` on authentik changes | Read-only plan, advisory |
{% if use_unifi %}| `unifi-drift-plan` | schedule, and `main` on unifi changes | Read-only plan, advisory; needs LAN reach to the console |
{% endif %}
The drift plans exist because every supervised module is applied by hand: a
console hot-fix would otherwise sit undetected until someone happened to run a
plan. They exit 2 on a non-empty diff and are `allow_failure: true`, so drift
shows as a warning rather than a broken pipeline.

## Backend seams

Only the backends the answers offer are implemented. Where a replacement would
have to plug in:

- **DNS** (`dns_backend`) — `cloudflare/` here, plus the cert-manager
  `ClusterIssuer` DNS-01 solver and the external-dns HelmRelease in
  `kubernetes/infrastructure/`. The CI jobs name the directory literally and are
  gated on the answer, so a second value renders no DNS pipeline rather than one
  pointing at a directory that does not exist.
- **SSO** — `authentik/` here, plus `kubernetes/apps/authentik` and the Traefik
  forward-auth middleware.
- **Secrets** (`secrets_backend`) — not a Terraform concern beyond the `op://`
  references in the Taskfile; the in-cluster half is the `ClusterSecretStore`.

The template's [ARCHITECTURE.md](https://git.ericsweiss.com/eric/weisssrv-cluster-template/-/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md) carries the full
seam description for each.
